<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pradyumna Shah</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1804">1804</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pradyumna Shah was the last king of independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/garhwal">Garhwal</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/himalayas">Himalayas</a> before the Gorkha conquest. After a Gorkha invasion in the early 1790s he agreed to pay tribute to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a>, and when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gurkhas">Gorkhas</a> invaded again in 1803 he fled his capital at Srinagar. He was killed at Khurbura near Dehradun in 1804, and Garhwal stayed under Gorkha rule until the Anglo-Nepalese War restored his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudarshan-shah">Sudarshan Shah</a> at Tehri.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pradyumna-shah.jpg" alt="Pradyumna Shah" width="274" height="319" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Detail_of_Raja_Pradyumna_Shah,_from_a_painting_of_Raja_Pradyumna_Shah_of_Garhwal_State_seated_with_his_younger_brother,_Parakram,_Garhwal,_ca.1785_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
